WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:05.010
The Appalachians and Ouachitas lie inland from today's passive margins, but

00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:07.530
their folds and thrusts record ancient compression.

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:09.630
Start with late Precambrian rifting.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:13.430
A continent separated from eastern North America, and spreading created an

00:00:13.430 --> 00:00:16.710
ocean. The continental edge then became a passive margin.

00:00:17.010 --> 00:00:19.690
The present coastline cannot tell you what kind of plate boundary

00:00:19.690 --> 00:00:22.430
occupied this region hundreds of millions of years ago.

00:00:23.020 --> 00:00:28.280
Later, oceanic lithosphere attached to North America subducted beneath an offshore

00:00:28.280 --> 00:00:32.940
island arc. As the ocean narrowed, the arc approached and collided

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:36.640
with the margin. Around four hundred fifty million years ago, that

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.240
collision produced the Taconic mountain-building event.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:44.040
Orogeny means mountain building; each named event represents a distinct part

00:00:44.040 --> 00:00:44.820
of the history.

00:00:45.470 --> 00:00:49.090
Around four hundred million years ago, Avalonia collided with eastern North

00:00:49.090 --> 00:00:50.910
America in the Acadian orogeny.

00:00:51.150 --> 00:00:54.570
Thrust sheets loaded and bent the crust, forming the foreland basin

00:00:54.570 --> 00:00:56.710
associated with the Catskill deposits.

00:00:56.950 --> 00:01:01.050
Distinguish this continental-fragment collision from the earlier island-arc collision.

00:01:01.330 --> 00:01:04.590
Both caused shortening, but the incoming crust and timing were different.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:08.860
In the late Paleozoic, roughly three hundred thirty to three hundred

00:01:08.860 --> 00:01:12.260
million years ago, Gondwana collided with North America.

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:16.340
The African and South American parts contributed to Appalachian and Ouachita

00:01:16.340 --> 00:01:19.980
mountain building. Large thrusts and folds stacked and shortened crust as

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:24.980
Pangaea assembled. Erosion later carried mountain-derived sediment far away, leaving datable

00:01:24.980 --> 00:01:27.120
mineral grains that help trace its source.

00:01:27.890 --> 00:01:31.590
By about two hundred twenty million years ago, extension began breaking

00:01:31.590 --> 00:01:36.590
the joined region apart. Fault-bounded basins accumulated red sediment and basalt.

00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.150
Jurassic spreading around one hundred eighty million years ago opened the

00:01:40.150 --> 00:01:44.690
Atlantic. The sequence is rifting, ocean opening, several collisions, then renewed

00:01:44.690 --> 00:01:48.770
rifting. Today's eroded mountains preserve the collision history even though the

00:01:48.770 --> 00:01:50.550
active boundary has moved offshore.
